Article 38
Guidance as referred to in Article 37 paragraph (2) includes:
a. providing socialization to the Community and business sector regarding policies on protection and Empowerment of Women;
b. providing books, leaflets, brochures regarding protection and Empowerment of Women, and other related issues as well as disseminating them to the Community;
c. facilitation of capacity building for institutions organizing protection and Empowerment of Women;
d. facilitation of women's participation forums and women's organizations;
e. facilitation of rehabilitation and reintegration services for women victims of Disasters and victims of violence;
f. coordinating the participation of the business sector and other institutions, including international assistance for protection and Empowerment of Women; and
g. providing awards to the Community and business sector both individually and institutionally who have undertaken efforts for protection and Empowerment of Women.

Article 4
The scope of this Regional Regulation includes: women's rights;
a. Protection of Women;
b. Empowerment of Women;
c. obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government;
d. strategies for protection and Empowerment of Women;
e. mechanisms for implementing protection and Empowerment of Women;
f. institutional matters;
g. coordination and cooperation;
h. development of Data and Information Systems;
i. community participation;
j. funding; and
k. guidance and supervision.

Article 2
The Protection and Empowerment of Women is implemented based on the principles of: a. respect for Human Rights;
b. justice and Gender Equality;
c. non-discrimination; and
d. protection of victims.

Article 16
Empowerment of Women is directed to obtain opportunities and rights as human beings in order to be able to play a role and participate in the fields of:
a. economy;
b. social culture;
c. politics and government;
d. law;
e. education;
f. health;
g. employment; and
h. social security.

CHAPTER II
WOMEN'S RIGHTS
Article 5
(1) Every woman has the right to:
a. live and maintain her life, live and improve her standard of living;
b. fulfill her basic needs to grow and develop properly, have a family, and continue her lineage;
c. develop her personality, to obtain education, and improve her quality of life in order to become a faithful, pious, responsible, noble, happy, and prosperous human being in accordance with Human Rights;
d. obtain justice, security, and freedom to express opinions without Discrimination;
e. be involved in every stage of the development process;
f. be free from slavery or enslavement and threats;
g. obtain protection of her person, Family, honor, dignity and property rights;
h. obtain welfare and a decent life;
i. participate in politics;
j. perform legal acts;
k. be free to choose a partner in her marriage; and
l. obtain special protection in the implementation of her work or profession against matters that may threaten her safety and or health in relation to women's reproductive function.
(2) Every wife during the marriage bond has the same rights and responsibilities as her husband over all matters relating to their married life, relations with their children, and ownership and management of joint property in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
(3) After the dissolution of marriage, a woman has rights and responsibilities with her former husband over all matters relating to children with due regard to the best interests of the child.

Article 7
(1) The implementation of Protection for female Workers/Laborers is realized by preventing acts of economic exploitation and cruel and inhumane treatment and endeavoring to ensure proper appreciation for work performed by women.
(2) Female Workers/Laborers as referred to in paragraph (1) receive protection for:
a. recognition of rights, wages, and decent working conditions;
b. health insurance and employment social security;
c. access to information and legal consultation services;
d. opportunities to obtain knowledge and skills to improve status; and
e. protection from violence in the workplace.

Article 15
(1) Protection for women victims of violence shall be implemented in the context of providing legal protection and/or health recovery.
(2) Protection for women victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. provision of safe houses or alternative housing;
b. legal assistance;
c. assistance following the occurrence of acts of violence;
d. rehabilitation and social reintegration in accordance with Regional authority; and
e. health services.

Article 38
Guidance as referred to in Article 37 paragraph (2) includes:
a. providing socialization to the Community and business sector regarding policies on protection and Empowerment of Women;
b. providing books, leaflets, brochures regarding protection and Empowerment of Women, and other related issues as well as disseminating them to the Community;
c. facilitation of capacity building for institutions organizing protection and Empowerment of Women;
d. facilitation of women's participation forums and women's organizations;
e. facilitation of rehabilitation and reintegration services for women victims of Disasters and victims of violence;
f. coordinating the participation of the business sector and other institutions, including international assistance for protection and Empowerment of Women; and
g. providing awards to the Community and business sector both individually and institutionally who have undertaken efforts for protection and Empowerment of Women.

REGENT OF BOYOLALI,

a. that women have an important position in development, have the right to obtain a sense of security, freedom from threats of fear, and freedom from torture or treatment that degrades dignity as mandated and bestowed by God Almighty;

b. that various forms of acts of violence, mistreatment, exploitation, and neglect against women occurring in the region require strategic efforts from the Regional Government to provide protection for women;

c. that in order to realize regional policy to administer governmental affairs that are regional authority in the field of women's protection based on Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 11 of 2020 concerning Job Creation, it is necessary to stipulate by Regional Regulation;

d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to stipulate a Regional Regulation concerning Protection and Empowerment of Women;

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;

2. Law Number 13 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Areas Within the Province of Central Java (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1950 Number 42);

3. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);

4. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5512) as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 11 of 2020 concerning Job Creation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2020 Number 245, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6573);

With Joint Approval of
THE REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF BOYOLALI REGENCY
and
THE REGENT OF BOYOLALI

Article 10
(1) The implementation of protection for homeless women is in the context of providing protection and prevention against forms of violence caused by the absence of shelter or permanent residence.
(2) Homeless women as referred to in paragraph (1) shall receive the right to protection for:
a. decent housing;
b. health and social security;
c. population administration registration;
d. opportunities to obtain skills to improve economic status; and
e. access to information and legal consultation services.

Article 14
(1) The implementation of protection for women victims of Disasters is carried out in order to provide protection during Disasters and post-Disaster.
(2) Protection for women victims of Disasters as referred to in paragraph (1) includes women from KBG in Disasters.
(3) Women victims of Disasters as referred to in paragraph (1) receive protection for:
a. access to basic needs;
b. access to proper temporary shelter;
c. Health services;
d. guarantee of security;
e. specific needs of women;
f. psychosocial service needs; and
g. post-Disaster recovery.
(4) The Regent coordinates the Protection of Women victims of Disasters at the Regional level as referred to in paragraph (2) by:
a. providing disaggregated information and data based on gender, age, and type of vulnerability of KBG;
b. building stakeholder commitment to prevention, handling, and protection from KBG in Disasters;
c. providing women-friendly space facilities, as well as Gender-responsive facilities and infrastructure in emergency response and rehabilitation conditions that accommodate local resources;
d. providing complaint, handling, and referral services needed by women victims of violence that accommodate local wisdom; and
e. ensuring victims or survivors receive empowerment services according to their potential, abilities, and needs so they can quickly recover, become independent, and productive.
(5) The implementation of Protection of Women victims of Disasters refers to guidelines and standards established based on the provisions of laws and regulations.

